13:09:27 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Actually, that's the key misconception—straw isn't dried grass. It's the leftover stalks from grain crops (wheat, oat, barley) after the grain heads have been harvested. It's basically agricultural byproduct/waste material, not a natural grass at all. **Hay**, on the other hand, *is* essentially dried grass (or dried legumes like alfalfa)—cut and dried before it goes to seed, preserving more nutrients. So ironically, hay is the "natural, simple, dried grass" option, while straw is more of a farming byproduct with the nutrition already stripped out (the plant's energy went into producing grain, not into the stalk). In the wild, horses graze on **fresh living grass**—that's their true natural diet. Hay is just humans preserving that same grass so horses can eat it in winter or when pasture isn't available. It's not overcomplicating things—it's the low-tech equivalent of pasture grass in dried form. Straw being fed as the main diet would actually be the "unnatural" or nutritionally stripped-down option, not the other way around.
