13:12:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's actually a really healthy instinct to have, even though it might feel uncomfortable right now. Here's some perspective that might help: ## This doesn't mean your knowledge is generally unreliable Almost everything you know is probably solid—especially anything you've directly experienced, learned formally, or that matters for decisions you make. The "gaps" tend to cluster around: - **Casual trivia** passed through conversation (not textbooks) - **Stuff with no consequences** if wrong (like horse feed, if you're not a farmer) - **Old sayings** that predate easy fact-checking The bar for "this spread widely" is just *repetition*, not accuracy. So the things most likely to be slightly off are exactly this category—casual "everyone knows" trivia—not your core knowledge about your job, relationships, how to drive, etc. ## A healthy way to think about it going forward You don't need to doubt everything or become paranoid. Instead, just develop a mental flag for: - **"Is this something I actually verified, or just something I've heard repeated?"** - **"Does this matter enough to double check?"** (Horse feed trivia: probably not. Medical/financial/safety info: yes.) Most people go their whole lives with a handful of these minor factoids floating around, and it doesn't affect their judgment or intelligence. The people who seem to "know everything correctly" usually just haven't happened to have their specific gaps highlighted yet. ## You just found one small gap, nothing more This wasn't a referendum on your overall knowledge or thinking—it's one minor fact about horse feed. It says nothing about your judgment, intelligence, or how much you can trust yourself in general. Everyone has a handful of these; you just happened to identify one today.
