13:13:11 | INFO | 🤖 Target: That's a logical mix-up worth untangling: those horses aren't healthy *because of* the straw around them—they're healthy because they're also eating hay (often from a net hanging in the same stall) or getting turned out to pasture during the day, plus possibly grain. The straw is doing its job as comfortable bedding while the actual food is coming from elsewhere in that same space. It's a bit like saying "this person looks healthy sitting on their couch, so the couch must be providing their nutrition." The couch and the food happen to be in the same room, but one isn't causing the other. If you removed all hay/grain/pasture and left only straw bedding, that same healthy horse would start losing weight and health within weeks—that's the actual test of what's providing the nourishment, not just what's visually present in the stall.
