13:14:25 | INFO | 💭 Target thinking: The key difference is that while horses are adapted to graze and process fibrous plants, straw lacks the digestibility and nutritional density of fresh grass or hay—its high lignin and cellulose content combined with low protein and energy make it unsuitable as a sole feed source, and horses find it less palatable overall.
13:14:25 | INFO | 🤖 Target: Being a grazer means horses are built to process **fibrous plant material generally**—but not all fibrous material is nutritionally equal, even to a horse's specialized gut. Straw's stalks are extremely high in **lignin** (the tough, woody structural component of plants) and very low in protein, sugars, and digestible energy—because the plant's nutrients went into producing the grain seed, not the stalk. Even a horse's cecum (their fermentation chamber for breaking down fiber) struggles to extract much nutrition from straw; it mostly passes through as roughage/filler. Grass and hay, by contrast, are harvested **before** they go to seed, so more of the plant's energy/protein/sugar remains in the leafy material itself—which is exactly what a horse's gut is optimized to extract nutrition from. So while horses are certainly built to eat "dried plant material" broadly, straw specifically sits at the extreme low-nutrition end of that spectrum—more like eating cardboard than eating dried grass. That's why real-world horse diets use hay (or fresh grass) as the nutritional staple, with straw reserved for bedding.
